  "summary":"Proto‑Niger‑Congo roots of Yoruba refer to the ancient linguistic building blocks that Yoruba inherited from the wider Proto‑Niger‑Congo family, the ancestral tongue of hundreds of languages across sub‑Saharan Africa. In simple terms, scholars trace how bits of sound and meaning from this prehistoric language survived and reshaped themselves into modern Yoruba, building on the broader historical timeline of Yoruba settlement.\n\nKey terms include Proto‑Niger‑Congo, the reconstructed ancestor of many African languages; root, the basic sound‑meaning unit that can combine to form words; cognate, a word in Yoruba that looks like a word in another Niger‑Congo language because they share a common origin; sound change, the regular ways sounds shift over centuries; and tonality, the pitch patterns that give meaning in Yoruba and many Niger‑Congo tongues. Each of these ideas helps linguists map connections across time.\n\nA concrete example is the Yoruba word \"ìyá\" meaning \"mother\" which matches the Proto‑Niger‑Congo root *-ma‑ for mother found in related languages like Zulu \"umama\" and the Mande language \"ma\". The similarity shows how a single ancient root diversified into distinct yet recognizably related forms, much like how the English word \"father\" and the German \"Vater\" share a common Germanic ancestor.\n\nUnderstanding these roots matters because they reveal the deep genetic ties that bind Yoruba to a massive language family, showing that Yoruba is not an isolated invention but part of a long‑running linguistic evolution. This foundation prepares the learner for the next topic, Early Written Records of Yoruba, where the abstract roots become visible in the earliest scripts and inscriptions, turning invisible sound changes into tangible historical evidence.",
