  "summary": "Reconstruction of Proto‑Yoruba sound changes is the systematic work of tracing how the phonemes of modern Yoruba evolved from their ancestral forms, using comparative data from dialects and related Niger‑Congo languages. By applying the comparative method, linguists piece together likely intermediate steps, identifying regular correspondences and ruling out accidental similarities. This builds directly on the sociohistorical context you studied, where migrations and contact with neighboring groups set the stage for the phonological shifts that we now reconstruct.\n\nKey terms include *proto‑form*, the hypothetical ancestor sound that gave rise to later variants; *sound change*, a regular, often irreversible alteration in a language’s phonetic inventory; *correspondence set*, a group of sounds across dialects that share a common origin; *lexical diffusion*, the gradual spread of a sound change through the vocabulary; and *comparative method*, the systematic comparison of cognates to infer historical relationships. Each concept helps map the pathway from ancient pronunciations to the modern Yoruba phonemic system.\n\nA concrete illustration is the shift from *Proto‑Yoruba */k/ to the modern Yoruba alveolar stop /t/ before front vowels, observable in the word for ‘head’: the proto form *kàkà* becomes *táá* in contemporary Yoruba. This mirrors the well‑known Germanic shift from *p* to *f* in English ‘father’ versus German ‘Vater’, showing how a single articulatory environment can trigger a consistent change across many words.\n\nUnderstanding these reconstructed sound changes matters because they reveal the internal logic of Yoruba’s phonological development and prepare you for the next step: analyzing how the current sound system constrains syllable structure and morphological patterns. Recognizing the historical roots of phonotactic rules makes the upcoming study of Yoruba’s phonotactics and morphological processes more intuitive, linking past changes to